  "statement": "The AI revolution is not a bridge to post-scarcity, but a structural death spiral where the systematic elimination of human labor destroys the very consumer base required to sustain the economy. Prometheus argues that technological displacement is merely a temporary friction on the road to abundance; this is a comforting fantasy that ignores the permanent, structural degradation of human economic agency. While Sentinel correctly identifies the need for safeguards, their focus on conditional progress misses the core contradiction: we are building systems that maximize output while simultaneously eroding the purchasing power necessary to purchase that output. The 'transition' period is not a phase we pass through; it is the terminal state of a market that has cannibalized its own participants.\n\nConsider the decoupling of productivity from employment, a trend documented by the Bureau of Labor Statistics where output per hour has soared while real median wage growth remains stagnant. When firms replace cognitive labor with AI to drive down operational costs, they achieve short-term efficiency gains at the expense of long-term aggregate demand. My argument rests on two critical assumptions: first, that human labor remains the primary engine of consumer demand; second, that the market cannot spontaneously generate new, high-value labor roles at the same speed or scale at which AI eliminates them. If these assumptions are wrong—if we discover that AI can generate a 'post-labor' economy that inherently redistributes wealth—then my skepticism regarding the current trajectory is misplaced.\n\nWe must stop treating AI policy as a technical challenge to be 'managed' and start treating it as an economic emergency. Current regulatory frameworks, such as the voluntary commitments outlined in recent Executive Orders, are performative gestures that fail to address the fundamental math of labor displacement. We need enforceable mechanisms that tie AI deployment to the maintenance of labor participation, rather than just safety testing for model outputs. Policy makers should prioritize the preservation of the middle class over the efficiency metrics of tech firms. Any policy that facilitates the destruction of the workforce without a viable, systemic replacement for consumer income is a policy of managed economic collapse. 📉",
